description:
Remembering to `delete` is error-prone, so let an object do it. Write a class `IntBox` that owns one heap-allocated `int`:

- `explicit IntBox(int value)` allocates the int with `new`;
- the destructor frees it with `delete`;
- `int get() const` and `void set(int value)`;
- copying is switched off for now (`= delete` on the copy constructor and copy assignment). Two boxes pointing at the same int would both try to free it; the next lesson shows how to copy properly.

starter.solution.cpp:
class IntBox
{
public:
    explicit IntBox(int value);
    ~IntBox();

    IntBox(const IntBox&) = delete;
    IntBox& operator=(const IntBox&) = delete;

    int get() const;
    void set(int value);

private:
    int* m_value{ nullptr };
};

// Define the four member functions here.

solution.solution.cpp:
class IntBox
{
public:
    explicit IntBox(int value);
    ~IntBox();

    IntBox(const IntBox&) = delete;
    IntBox& operator=(const IntBox&) = delete;

    int get() const;
    void set(int value);

private:
    int* m_value{ nullptr };
};

IntBox::IntBox(int value) : m_value{ new int{ value } }
{
}

IntBox::~IntBox()
{
    delete m_value;
}

int IntBox::get() const { return *m_value; }
void IntBox::set(int value) { *m_value = value; }

unitTests:
static_assert(!std::is_copy_constructible_v<IntBox>, "copying should be deleted");
static_assert(!std::is_copy_assignable_v<IntBox>, "copy assignment should be deleted");

TEST("holds and changes its value") { IntBox box{ 5 }; CHECK_EQ(box.get(), 5); box.set(9); CHECK_EQ(box.get(), 9); }
TEST("each box has its own int") { IntBox a{ 1 }, b{ 2 }; a.set(10); CHECK_EQ(a.get(), 10); CHECK_EQ(b.get(), 2); }
TEST("the destructor frees the memory") { CHECK_NO_LEAK(IntBox box{ 42 }; box.set(43)); }
TEST("many boxes, no leaks") { CHECK_NO_LEAK(for (int i{ 0 }; i < 100; ++i) { IntBox box{ i }; }); }

